// A nested loop is a separate iteration space, so the outer body has no
// dependence answer the analysis can use.
//
// `s` is the only chain that actually crosses the outer iteration. `j` is read
// by the inner condition and by the `if` below, and written by the inner step, so
// a walk of the outer body sees it read and written -- but it is reset to 0 by
// the inner `for` header on every outer pass, so it carries nothing between outer
// iterations. Counting it would make this look like two independent chains and
// unroll it. Left rolled.
//
// The `break` is what makes this probe load-bearing: without it the inner loop
// folds away and the shape rolls either way, so removing the rule would not fail
// anything. tests/cases/unroll_dep.z checks that the inner loop of a parallel
// shape still unrolls on its own account.
int main() {
    var s = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) {
        for (var j = 0; j < 10; j = j + 1) {
            if (j == 2) { break; }
            s = s + 1;
        }
    }
    print(s);
    return 0;
}
